  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington ran hot yesterday despite Columbus Day headwinds\u2014a genuine win. Yesterday's $3,046 revenue with five groomers produced $485 per groomer, 3% above your trailing-30-day baseline of $472. Core grooming revenue jumped 33% week-over-week ($2,485 vs $1,810), driven by higher transaction volume (36 visits vs 30). The team executed well. However, retail cratered 32% ($366 vs $541)\u2014this is your one concern to address. Month-to-date, Port Washington sits at $36,745 against a DOW-adjusted expectation of $39,279, a real 6.5% miss. Add-on attach held steady at 62%, so the retail drop is pure front-of-house focus loss, not a grooming quality signal. Your year-to-date momentum remains exceptional: up 39% revenue and 38% appointments versus last year.\n\nHicksville is flashing a warning. Yesterday's 34% revenue drop ($1,152 vs $1,738) looks worse than it is\u2014only two groomers worked, producing $480 per groomer, well above the 30-day baseline of $402. The real problem is this week: WTD revenue down 47% and appointments down 64% (8 visits vs 22). This is not Columbus Day; this is capacity or booking discipline deterioration. Add-on attach actually improved to 50% from 29%, so upselling held, but core groom volume collapsed. Retail down 28% compounds the issue. Month-to-date Hicksville is flat against DOW-adjusted expectations, but the momentum is concerning and WTD data suggests deeper staffing or pipeline problems emerging.\n\nGlen Cove remains a rounding error and a liability. One groomer, zero bathers, zero appointments scheduled for the next week. Yesterday's 55% revenue drop and 74% grooming collapse reflects a store with no capacity footprint. You are carrying this location; the question is whether it's a build or a close.\n\nPort Washington today: 7 groomer-days with 15 bather hours; tomorrow 6 groomer-days with 15 bather hours; next 7 days 35 groomer-days and 83 bather hours scheduled."
